# udev rule for the wheel-button dongle (untested: no dongle, no Pi at the time of writing).
#
# TEMPLATE: pi/install.sh writes /etc/udev/rules.d/70-revview-dongle.rules from this file on every run, with
# @VENDOR@ and @PRODUCT@ replaced by `device.vendor` and `device.product` of /etc/revview/buttons.json. The ids
# come from that file: edit it (read the real ones with `lsusb`), not this one nor the generated rule, and run
# install.sh again (bench items W1, D5 and D13). The default ids there, ZMK's 1d50:615e, were written from memory
# and not read from a real dongle.
#
# What it does:
# - gives the event nodes of this USB device to group "input", mode 0660, so the server's user (in group
#   "input", see revview.service) can read and grab them without root. On a stock Raspberry Pi OS the
#   default rules already do this for all event nodes; this makes it explicit for this device.
# - adds an optional stable symlink /dev/input/revview-dongle on the keyboard node. The server does not
#   need it: it finds the dongle by vendor/product in /proc/bus/input/devices (src/input/devices.js). Use it
#   only as a fallback with "device": {"path": "/dev/input/revview-dongle"} in the config. A dongle can show
#   several event nodes (keyboard, consumer control, mouse); the symlink is meant for the one flagged as
#   keyboard and may be wrong if that flag is not set. Check with `ls -l /dev/input/` (bench D5).
#
# The file name starts with 70 so it runs after 60-input-id.rules, which sets ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD.

SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event[0-9]*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="@VENDOR@", ATTRS{idProduct}=="@PRODUCT@", GROUP="input", MODE="0660"
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event[0-9]*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="@VENDOR@", ATTRS{idProduct}=="@PRODUCT@", ENV{ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD}=="1", SYMLINK+="input/revview-dongle"
